# What this repo grants itself (`.:V13`). The tool gates its own tree, so
# this file is the dogfood: every grant here is one a user could write.

# The floor. Source, config and data files are plain ASCII, which is the
# strict default V1 would give them anyway -- stated because a reader
# should not have to know that to read this file.
* ascii

# The specs and their archives: prose that DOCUMENTS character sets, so it
# holds the characters it documents. `spec` is declared in `.ctrm-sets`
# beside this file.
*.md ascii+spec

# The prose a READER meets first is held to the strict default, narrower
# than the specs beside it. Nothing in a README or a notices file needs a
# character outside ASCII, and a tool arguing for plain text whose own
# front page is full of curly quotes is arguing against itself.
#
# These lines sit AFTER the one above and so override it (V2, last match
# wins) -- which is the per-file-beats-per-type case, dogfooded here
# rather than only tested.
README.md ascii
LICENSE ascii

# AGENTS.md is the one exception among them: its glossary teaches the spec
# notation, so it prints the handful of logic symbols the specs are written
# in. `caveman` is exactly that notation and nothing wider -- no curly
# quotes, no dashes -- so the rest of the file stays as plain as before.
AGENTS.md ascii+caveman
docs/**/*.md ascii

# The token ceilings, which cite spec sections by the section sign.
.context-limits ascii+caveman

# VENDORED, not ours to rewrite: the upstream `hk` Pkl schema, kept here so
# the gate runs offline (`.:C`). Granting `any` is the honest spelling for
# a file this repo does not author -- narrowing it would mean editing
# someone else's document to fit our rule.
pkl/Config.pkl any
